# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hydromart — PRODUCTION secrets & tunables for the Docker Compose deploy.
#
#   cp .env.production.example .env      # docker compose auto-loads ./.env
#   # then fill in every value marked REQUIRED below.
#
# NEVER commit the real .env. All REQUIRED values are read by
# docker-compose.prod.yml via ${VAR:?...} and the deploy will refuse to
# start if any are unset.
#
# Generate ALL secret values at once with:
#   bash scripts/generate-secrets.sh
# (or one at a time: openssl rand -hex 32 — 64 chars, above every minimum).
# Services boot-fail in production if any secret is missing OR still a
# "change-me" dev placeholder (packages/platform requiredSecret guard).
# ------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---- REQUIRED: database ----
# Password for the `hydromart` Postgres superuser. Used both by the postgres
# container and inside every service's <SVC>_DATABASE_URL.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# ---- REQUIRED: auth / JWT ----
# Access token signing secret (min 32 chars). openssl rand -hex 32
JWT_ACCESS_SECRET=
# Refresh token signing secret (min 32 chars, different from the access one).
JWT_REFRESH_SECRET=

# ---- REQUIRED: internal service-to-service auth ----
# Shared key every service uses to authenticate internal calls (notifications,
# payment->order confirm). Must be identical across all services (it is — one var).
INTERNAL_SERVICE_KEY=

# ---- REQUIRED: payment webhook ----
# HMAC-SHA256 secret the payment gateway signs webhook callbacks with (min 16 chars).
PAYMENT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# ---- Partner integration (H-30) — no env needed, listed so it is not a surprise ----
# API keys minted in /hq/api-keys authenticate partner calls to /api/v1/partner/*
# via the `x-api-key` header, with per-route scopes (webhooks:read, webhooks:write).
# Webhook endpoints registered in /hq/webhooks receive a signed POST per subscribed
# event; the scheduler container drains the queue every 5 minutes (scripts/scheduler/
# crontab), retrying with backoff and giving up after six attempts.
#
# Receivers verify with: HMAC-SHA256(secret, "<X-Hydromart-Timestamp>.<raw body>")
# compared against X-Hydromart-Signature (`sha256=<hex>`).

# ---- REQUIRED: HR biometrics ----
# AES-256-GCM key the enrolled face templates are encrypted with at rest. A face
# cannot be reissued after a leak, so hr-service refuses to boot without this in
# production. openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Rotating it does NOT re-encrypt anything: every existing enrolment becomes
# undecryptable and those employees must enroll again.
HR_FACE_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# ---- REQUIRED: OTP ----
# Server-side pepper mixed into OTP hashing. openssl rand -hex 32
OTP_PEPPER=

# ---- OPTIONAL: Google Play reviewer access (J6) ----
# A Play reviewer is in another country and will never receive an Indonesian SMS. Without
# a way in they cannot open an app that is entirely behind a login, and "we could not sign
# in" is the most common rejection there is.
#
# These phone numbers get a FIXED code instead of a random one, and are not sent an SMS —
# the reviewer already has the code from the Play listing, and the demo number may not be
# a SIM you hold. Everything else is unchanged: the code is still hashed, still expires,
# still single-use, still attempt-limited. There is no shortcut in the verify path.
#
# Anyone who learns the pair can sign in as those accounts, so point them at DEDICATED
# demo accounts — no real orders, not a real person's number. Rotate the code after
# review, or blank both to switch the feature off entirely.
# Setting the phone without the code fails auth-service at boot, on purpose.
#
# Comma-separated, one number per binary: the customer app needs a CUSTOMER account and
# Ops needs a staff one, and a phone carries a single role. Both listed = both apps can
# be in review at the same time. Put the staff account on a DEMO depot — on a production
# depot the reviewer reads live customer names, addresses and phone numbers.
#   REVIEWER_PHONE=+6281100000098,+6281100000099
REVIEWER_PHONE=
REVIEWER_OTP_CODE=

# Delivery channel: zenziva | sms. `console` ONLY writes the code to the auth
# container log — anyone who can read logs could then sign in as any customer —
# so it is a development channel and auth-service REFUSES TO BOOT on it when
# NODE_ENV=production. Set a real channel plus its credentials below.
#
# zenziva — the provider in use. Userkey + API Key come from the Zenziva console's
# "HTTP API" panel; the sender mask is configured there, not here. Copy is in
# zenziva-otp-delivery.adapter.ts (separate per registration / login).
#
# sms — generic token-authenticated JSON gateway, kept for a future switch.
OTP_DELIVERY_CHANNEL=zenziva
ZENZIVA_BASE_URL=https://console.zenziva.net
ZENZIVA_USERKEY=
ZENZIVA_PASSKEY=
SMS_API_BASE_URL=
SMS_API_TOKEN=
SMS_SENDER_ID=HYDROMART

# ---- REQUIRED: web app (baked into the client bundle at build time) ----
# The PUBLIC URL visitors' browsers use to reach the API gateway — NOT an internal
# docker hostname. It is frozen into the JS bundle at `docker compose build`, so
# set it correctly BEFORE building (changing it later needs a rebuild). Use your
# real domain behind TLS (https://api.your-domain.com) or, for a bare-IP deploy,
# http://<your-vps-public-ip>:8080. The build FAILS if this is unset.
PUBLIC_API_URL=
# Browser origin(s) allowed to call the gateway API (comma-separated, no trailing
# slash). MUST match where the web app is served or CORS blocks every fetch.
# With TLS: https://<WEB_DOMAIN>. Bare-IP: http://<your-vps-public-ip>:3000.
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# ---- OPTIONAL: TLS reverse proxy (only used with `--profile tls`) ----
# Public hostnames that resolve (DNS A/AAAA) to this VPS. Caddy auto-provisions
# Let's Encrypt certs for both. Leave blank if you are not running the tls profile.
# When you DO use TLS, set PUBLIC_API_URL above to https://<API_DOMAIN>.
WEB_DOMAIN=hydromart-digital.com
API_DOMAIN=

# ---- Prisma connection pool per service (B-12) ----
# Connections EACH service may hold. Default 5; with 16 service databases that is 80 of
# postgres's 150, leaving room for psql, migrations, backup-db.sh and the exporters.
# Unset it and Prisma reverts to (cpus*2+1) per service — ~272 on this box against a
# server ceiling of 150, which surfaces as random "can't reach database" errors under load.
# Raising this past 9 breaks the headroom check in scripts/lib/deploy-common.test.sh.
DB_POOL=

# ---- Where the web + gateway ports are published (H-19) ----
# Default (unset) is 127.0.0.1: the app and API are reachable only from the box itself,
# so the TLS reverse proxy is the sole public entrance. Keep it that way whenever you run
# `--profile tls` — the gateway now trusts X-Forwarded-For for rate limiting, and a
# directly reachable port lets a client spoof that header and bypass the limit.
#
# ONLY set this to 0.0.0.0 if you deploy WITHOUT a proxy (bare-IP, no WEB_DOMAIN). On that
# path nothing else publishes the app, so leaving it unset makes the site unreachable.
PUBLIC_BIND=

# ---- OPTIONAL: Swagger /docs in production (M12-11) ----
# Every service image runs with NODE_ENV=production, and in production /docs is NOT
# mounted at all unless BOTH of these are set — forgetting a password fails closed
# (no docs) rather than open (public API map). Both are now in the shared compose anchor,
# so setting them here reaches every service at once; leaving them blank keeps docs off.
# Basic auth over the public internet, so use a long random password:
#   DOCS_PASSWORD=$(openssl rand -base64 24)
# DOCS_USER=hydromart
# DOCS_PASSWORD=

# ---- REQUIRED: object storage (BiznetGio NEO / any S3-compatible) ----
# Shared endpoint/region/driver for every service that stores files. Per NEO's
# per-bucket keys, each service has its OWN bucket + access key below.
STORAGE_DRIVER=s3
STORAGE_S3_ENDPOINT=https://nos.jkt-1.neo.id
STORAGE_S3_REGION=jkt-1
# product images (product-service)
PRODUCT_STORAGE_S3_BUCKET=hydromart-products
PRODUCT_STORAGE_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
PRODUCT_STORAGE_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
PRODUCT_STORAGE_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://nos.jkt-1.neo.id/hydromart-products
# proof-of-delivery photos (delivery-service)
DELIVERY_STORAGE_S3_BUCKET=hydromart-pod
DELIVERY_STORAGE_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
DELIVERY_STORAGE_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
DELIVERY_STORAGE_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://nos.jkt-1.neo.id/hydromart-pod
# customer avatar photos (auth-service). Reuse the product bucket+creds if you
# prefer one bucket — avatars go under the avatars/ prefix, no collision.
AUTH_STORAGE_S3_BUCKET=hydromart-avatars
AUTH_STORAGE_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
AUTH_STORAGE_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
AUTH_STORAGE_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://nos.jkt-1.neo.id/hydromart-avatars
# agen registration photos (customer-service, SOP §7). OPTIONAL: leave all four blank and
# customer-service reuses the AUTH bucket above — agen photos go under the resellers/
# prefix, avatars under avatars/, so they cannot collide. Fill all four (not some) to give
# them their own bucket; a bucket paired with another bucket public URL serves 404s.
CUSTOMER_STORAGE_S3_BUCKET=
CUSTOMER_STORAGE_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
CUSTOMER_STORAGE_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
CUSTOMER_STORAGE_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=
# HR face frames: the punch photo kept as attendance evidence, plus the enrollment
# source photo. Biometric personal data — keep this bucket private-by-default and let
# the HR retention purge own its lifecycle. Leaving the bucket blank while
# HR_STORAGE_DRIVER=s3 makes every punch fail, not just the photo.
# Note the different endpoint: this bucket lives in wjv-1, not the jkt-1 the others use.
HR_STORAGE_S3_ENDPOINT=https://nos.wjv-1.neo.id
HR_STORAGE_S3_REGION=idn
HR_STORAGE_S3_BUCKET=hydromart-facer
HR_STORAGE_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
HR_STORAGE_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
HR_STORAGE_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://nos.wjv-1.neo.id/hydromart-facer

# ---- OPTIONAL: payment gateway (online charges) ----
# Blank = online charges fail closed (COD / manual confirm still work).
PAYMENT_GATEWAY_BASE_URL=
PAYMENT_GATEWAY_API_KEY=

# ---- OPTIONAL: error alerting ----
# Incoming webhook that receives a ping on every unhandled 5xx (route + stack).
# Works with Slack ("Incoming Webhooks") or Discord (channel webhook + `/slack`
# suffix, or plain — both payload keys are sent). Blank = disabled, no pings.
# Repeats of the same error are throttled (default 60s; tune ALERT_DEDUPE_SECONDS).
ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL=
# ALERT_DEDUPE_SECONDS=60

# ---- OPTIONAL: Sentry (error aggregation, PR-I) ----
# Blank = off, and off means OFF: the backend never calls Sentry.init and the web app
# never even downloads the SDK. Both halves redact before sending (the same
# redactAlertText the chat alerter uses; breadcrumbs and query strings are dropped),
# because a stack and a screen both carry customers' phone numbers.
SENTRY_DSN=
# The browser half is a separate DSN-shaped value because it is PUBLIC — it ships inside
# the bundle and inside both APKs. Never put the backend DSN here.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_ENV=production

# ---- OPTIONAL: WhatsApp (CRM broadcasts + depot low-stock alerts) ----
# Blank = console/log fallback, no real messages are sent.
WHATSAPP_API_URL=
WHATSAPP_API_TOKEN=
# Ops/warehouse WhatsApp number for depot low-stock alerts. Blank = disabled.
DEPOT_ALERT_PHONE=
# Per-gallon deposit (IDR) refunded on a courier-recorded return (design 2e).
GALLON_DEPOSIT_IDR=20000

# ---- OPTIONAL: Web Push (browser notifications, design 7b) ----
# Blank = push disabled; the app still stores notifications + sends WhatsApp.
# Generate a keypair once with:  npm run gen:vapid
# Keep the PRIVATE key secret — never commit a filled-in .env.
VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:ops@hydromart.id

# ---- OPTIONAL: FCM (Android push, F4) ----
# Blank = Android push disabled; browser push and stored notifications are unaffected.
# From the Firebase service-account JSON (Project settings → Service accounts → Generate
# new private key). ONE Firebase project holds both Android apps — id.hydromart.app and
# id.hydromart.ops — so there is only ever one set of these values.
#
# FCM_PRIVATE_KEY must be the PEM on a SINGLE LINE with its newlines written as the two
# characters \n. A real newline here silently truncates the value at the first one, and
# the symptom is every Android push failing with an unusable-key error.
FCM_PROJECT_ID=
FCM_CLIENT_EMAIL=
FCM_PRIVATE_KEY=

# ---- OPTIONAL: where alert notifications link to (N12) ----
# Alertmanager builds the link in every notification from this. Unset, it uses the
# container's own hostname — a URL Discord refuses outright and no person can open, which
# is why alert links were empty. An SSH-tunnelled localhost is a legitimate answer here.
ALERTMANAGER_EXTERNAL_URL=http://localhost:9093

# How many days before a TLS certificate expires the weekly check starts shouting (N13).
TLS_WARN_DAYS=21

# ---- OPTIONAL: Android kill switch (F5) ----
# The lowest Android versionCode still allowed to run. 0 = nobody is blocked, which is
# where this normally sits. Raise it and restart the gateway to stop a shipped build —
# a broken checkout, a build leaking something it should not — in minutes, instead of
# waiting for every user to update on their own.
#
# The affected users get a blocking screen with a Play Store button and nothing else,
# so raise it only for a version that genuinely must not keep running.
MOBILE_MIN_VERSION_CODE=0
# N5: aim it. Two binaries are built from the same run and carry the SAME versionCode
# series, so the line above could only stop both at once — raising it to kill a broken
# customer release also killed every courier mid-delivery and every cashier mid-shift.
# Per-package floors, comma separated, and anything not named here uses the line above:
#   MOBILE_MIN_VERSION_CODE_BY_ID=id.hydromart.app=1200,id.hydromart.ops=0
MOBILE_MIN_VERSION_CODE_BY_ID=

# ---- OPTIONAL: client error reporting (N2) ----
# The web and mobile clients inline this at BUILD time, so it is a rebuild to change and
# an empty value means the SDK is never downloaded and nothing is sent. Until this was
# wired, the reporter existed in the source and in NO artefact we ship: server 5xx were
# reported and everything a user actually sees was invisible.
# SENTRY_DSN_WEB is a build arg (docker compose build), not a runtime variable.
SENTRY_DSN_WEB=
SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=production
# Where the browser is allowed to POST them. Caddy's CSP blocks anything not named here;
# the default covers Sentry SaaS. Self-hosted needs its own origin.
SENTRY_INGEST_ORIGIN=https://*.ingest.sentry.io

MOBILE_UPDATE_MESSAGE=

# ---- OPTIONAL: image registry (H-31/H-35) ----
# Leave BOTH commented out and the deploy behaves exactly as it always has: images are
# built on this box by scripts/rebuild-stale.sh and tagged hydromart-<svc>:local.
#
# Set IMAGE_PREFIX and the deploy PULLS instead of building — no 1.5 GB build peak beside
# Postgres, no image layers on the production disk, and no repeat of the BuildKit daemon
# panic that stopped all 25 containers on 2026-08-02. The .github/workflows/images.yml
# workflow publishes one image per service, tagged with the commit SHA, on every green CI.
#
# IMAGE_TAG is NOT set here: deploy.sh and rollback.sh set it to the commit they are
# deploying, and everything else (the watchdog's converge) derives it from HEAD.
# The box must be logged in first:  docker login ghcr.io -u <user>
#
# IMAGE_PREFIX=ghcr.io/<owner>/<repo>-

# ---- OPTIONAL: deploy behaviour ----
# B-20: what a deploy does when the incoming commit carries migrations.
#   apply (default) — run them before any container starts on the new code
#   stop            — refuse the deploy and leave the running stack alone
# DEPLOY_MIGRATE=apply

# One business timezone for the whole platform (H-16): day/month boundaries in every
# service, and the scheduler's cron clock. Leave blank to accept Asia/Jakarta.
PRICING_TZ=Asia/Jakarta

# Refunds strictly above this amount (IDR) park in the HQ approval queue instead of
# settling immediately (feature 14a / Q-11). Blank = Rp 100.000.
REFUND_HQ_THRESHOLD=100000
